<article class="env-definition" id="53F1FD">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/53F1FD">Definition <span data-tag="53F1FD">8.1.3.4</span>.</a> A monoidal category $(\calC ,\otimes )$ is said to be <em>left closed</em> if, for each $A \in \calC $, the functor </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  N \mapsto A \otimes N \]
</div>
  <p> admits a right adjoint </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  Y \mapsto {}^ A\! Y. \]
</div>
  <p> We say that $(\calC , \otimes )$ is <em>right-closed</em> if the opposite monoidal structure $(\calC , \otimes ^{op})$ is left-closed; in other words, if every functor </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  N \mapsto N \otimes A \]
</div>
  <p> has a right adjoint </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  Y \mapsto Y^ A. \]
</div>
  <p> Finally, we say that $(\calC , \otimes )$ is <em>closed</em> if it is both right-closed and left-closed.monoidal category!left closedmonoidal category!right closedmonoidal category!closed </p>
